1 )  Explain in detail about Agile Methodology :


What is Agile?
Agile is a software development methodology that focuses on delivering software in small, 
incremental parts rather than building the entire product at once. 
Instead of following one long rigid plan, the project is broken into small cycles called Sprints (usually 1-4 weeks each), 
and after every sprint, a working piece of software is delivered.
The core idea: build, get feedback, improve, repeat — instead of waiting until the very end to show the client anything.

Why Agile Came into Existence

Before Agile, most projects used the Waterfall Model, where:
Requirements → Design → Development → Testing → Deployment happened in strict sequence
Testing happened only at the very end
If the client wanted a change, or if a mistake was found late, it was very costly and time-consuming to fix .
Agile was created to solve these problems by making development flexible, collaborative, and continuous .


Core Values of Agile (Agile Manifesto)
Agile is based on 4 key values:

Individuals and interactions over processes and tools
Working software over comprehensive documentation
Customer collaboration over contract negotiation
Responding to change over following a fixed plan.

Key Roles in Agile (Scrum Framework)

Product Owner – Represents the customer/business, manages the product backlog, decides priorities
Scrum Master – Facilitates the process, removes obstacles/blockers for the team, ensures Agile principles are followed
Development Team (includes testers, developers) – Actually builds and tests the product.

How Testing Fits into Agile
This is very relevant for you as a QA learner:

Testing is not a separate final phase — it happens continuously, within each sprint
Testers are involved from day one, along with developers, not after development is "complete"
Concepts like Continuous Integration/Continuous Testing are heavily used
Automation testing (like your Selenium learning) becomes very important in Agile, because features need to be tested quickly and repeatedly across many sprints .

Agile vs Waterfall (Quick Comparison)
         
         
      Agile                                                                          Waterfall

Flexible, iterative                                                                Fixed, sequential
Testing happens throughout                                                         Testing happens at the end
Changes welcomed anytime                                                           Changes are difficult once started
Faster feedback and delivery                                                       Feedback only after full completion
Customer involved continuously                                                     Customer involved mainly at start/end

Simple Example to Understand Agile
Imagine building a house:

Waterfall: You build the entire house, then show it to the owner. 
If they don't like the kitchen design, you have to redo a lot of work.

Agile: You build one room first (say, the kitchen), show it to the owner, get feedback, fix anything needed, then move to the next room. 
Mistakes get caught early, and the owner sees progress constantly.


2) Explain about Monitoring Activity :

   In software testing, Monitoring (often called Test Monitoring and Control) is an ongoing activity that runs throughout the entire testing process — from planning until test closure. 
   It's not a single phase like Test Planning or Test Execution; instead, it happens continuously alongside all other testing activities.

What is Monitoring Activity?
It is the process of tracking the progress of testing activities, comparing actual progress against the test plan, and taking corrective actions if things go off track.
Simply put: "Are we testing as planned? If not, what do we need to fix?" 

Purpose of Monitoring Activity

To track whether testing is progressing as per schedule
To identify risks or issues early, before they become big problems
To ensure test coverage is adequate
To measure test execution progress (how many test cases passed/failed/blocked)
To take corrective actions when deviations are found (e.g., delays, resource shortage, environment issues).

Core Values of Agile (Agile Manifesto)
Agile is based on 4 key values:

Individuals and interactions over processes and tools
Working software over comprehensive documentation
Customer collaboration over contract negotiation
Responding to change over following a fixed plan

How is Monitoring Done? (Common Methods/Tools)

Test Status Reports – Daily/weekly reports showing test execution status
Dashboards – Visual tracking (e.g., in Jira, TestRail, Zephyr) showing pass/fail metrics
Defect Reports – Tracking open, closed, reopened bugs
Metrics – e.g., Test case execution %, defect density, defect leakage.


Where Does This Fit in STLC?
In the Software Testing Life Cycle (STLC), Test Monitoring and Control isn't a separate one-time phase — it runs parallel to all phases:


 Requirement Analysis → Test Planning → Test Case Development → 
Test Environment Setup → Test Execution → Test Closure
                    ↑
        (Monitoring & Control happens throughout all of this)



Simple Example
Imagine your test plan says: "Execute 100 test cases in 5 days."

Day 3 (Monitoring): You check status and see only 40 test cases have been executed instead of the expected 60.
Action (Controlling): You investigate why — maybe the environment was down for a day — and then decide to extend testing hours or bring in an extra tester to catch up.

That combination of checking progress + fixing the gap is exactly what Monitoring Activity means in testing.
   









